# Works built on works: Little Women
#
# Louisa May Alcott's novel and what has grown from it, as an example of the
# relationships between works and between expressions. Most publications are in
# English, with German and French examples. Notes are in English. Each
# relationship is stated in one direction only; the inverse is inferred.
#
# Parts and sequels
#   Little Women was published in two volumes, 1868 and 1869 (in Britain the
#   second was called Good Wives). Here the novel is one work with the two
#   volumes as parts (rdawo:P10147). Many catalogues treat Good Wives as a
#   sequel instead: a point for discussion. Little Men (1871) and Jo's Boys
#   (1886) are sequels (rdawo:P10020, has sequel work).
#
# Expressions of the novel
#   - the English text of 1868-1869, and the 1880 one-volume edition, whose
#     text was revised (rdaeo:P20189, is revision of)
#   - translations (rdaeo:P20141): French by Anne Joba and by Paulette
#     Vielhomme-Callais, German by Monika Baark and by Bettina Münch (two
#     volumes, one per part)
#   - free translations (rdaeo:P20140): P.-J. Stahl's French version of 1880,
#     and Inge M. Artl's German version for children
#   - abridgements (rdaeo:P20121): a Macmillan reader retold by Anne Collins,
#     and Malika Ferdjoukh's abridged French translation (translation and
#     abridgement at once: which relationship?)
#   - audiobooks: English read by Laural Merlington, German read by Marie
#     Bierstedt (Artl's text)
#
# Works derived from the novel (work level)
#   - motion pictures 1933, 1949, 1994 and 2019 (rdawo:P10129). The 1949 film
#     is a remake of the 1933 film (rdawo:P10185) and shares its
#     screenwriters.
#   - television: BBC 2017 (rdawo:P10132), and the Japanese anime series
#     Ai no Wakakusa Monogatari, 1987
#   - graphic novel: Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy, 2019, a modern retelling
#     (rdawo:P10252)
#   - parody: Little Vampire Women, 2010 (rdawo:P10197)
#   - inspired by: March, Geraldine Brooks, 2005, the story of the absent
#     father (rdawo:P10290)
#   - commentary: The Annotated Little Women, John Matteson (rdawo:P10187)
#
# Adaptations stated between expressions
#   The musical (2005) and Mark Adamo's opera (1998) are linked to the English
#   text with the expression relationships rdaeo:P20239 (musical theatre
#   adaptation of) and rdaeo:P20240 (opera adaptation of), since the profile
#   has no work-level relationship for them.
#
# Things to check or add, as exercises
#   - which text (1868-1869 or 1880) the modern editions and the audiobook
#     follow; they are linked to the 1868-1869 text here
#   - the DVD in the French Folio junior edition holds the 1994 film; whether
#     it is the original or a French dubbed version (a new expression) is not
#     recorded
#   - manifestations of the films, the TV series, the anime and the musical
#   - Good Wives as a manifestation of part two
#
# Sources: Library of Congress and library catalogues for the English editions,
# BnF for the French, Deutsche Nationalbibliothek for the German. Identifiers
# are ad hoc (http://example.org/littlewomen/...).

